From d620bdd84868de9860245b36214fe1dc9fa1739b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 04:32:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gui: address cairo pixel bytes by host byte order

cairo stores ARGB32 and RGB24 pixels as native-endian 32-bit words, so
the byte holding a channel is B,G,R,A on little-endian hosts and A,R,G,B
on big-endian ones. gamma, the 8-bit export conversions, thumbnail
transfer, focus peaking, the cairo-to-pixbuf helper, the lcms display
transforms and the modules that read cairo surfaces back into the image
(watermark, overlay) all hard-coded the little-endian layout, as did a
few GUI-only buffers.

Add DT_CAIRO_RGB(c) and DT_CAIRO_ALPHA to dttypes.h and use them
wherever a cairo pixel or the 8-bit pixelpipe output is addressed byte
by byte; focus peaking writes packed words instead. The lcms transforms
feeding cairo use TYPE_ARGB_8 on big-endian hosts. Little-endian output
is unchanged byte for byte.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XfSsLvzPAMMJrirjDuBXbx
